[DOCUMENT]
TITLE: The cheap rebuild accepts deleting a node the canon still links to

[GRAMMAR]
IMPORT_FROM_FILE: @repo

[EVIDENCE]
UID: EV-CHEAP-REBUILD-SKIPS-INLINE-LINKS
TITLE: The cheap rebuild accepts deleting a node the canon still links to
DEPTH: implemented
AUTHORED_BY: llm
SOURCE: live 377-node canon plus three rolled-back mutations on an isolated copy; strictdoc 0.28.3; 2026-08-31
STATEMENT: >>>
The cheap rebuild over already-parsed documents does not validate inline link
references, and the earlier design rested on the belief that it did.

Measured on the live 377-node canon, twice independently and then confirmed
directly: after a full load pending_inline_links is 0 and 242 nodes carry
incoming links; after a rebuild over the same held documents it is 579 and
zero nodes do. The corpus holds 579 inline links across 59 files and 10 UIDs
that nothing but an inline link names.

Consequences measured, each rolled back: deleting a node referenced only by an
inline link PASSES the cheap rebuild in 0.021 s and is caught by the full
reload in 0.69 s; a statement gaining a link to a nonexistent UID PASSES
cheap and is caught by full; a UID rename on an inline-referenced node does
the same. The verb writing prose is the most common one, so it was also the
worst affected.

The cause is structural, not a bug: the drain and check are in the outer
create() at traceability_index_builder.py:266-278, while the append is at
:643, inside the half the cheap rebuild runs.
<<<
